 They afterwards offered up many victims. (Calmet)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Glory. The same word was before rendered majesty. (Haydock)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Victims, by the hands of the priests: or they slew them themselves, and presented the blood and fat to be placed on the altar by the priests."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Rams, including sheep and goats. — People, each in their proper sphere. The priests had the chief part, as the ministers of religion. The solemnity continued for a week, and many altars were erected in the court. (Calmet)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Which music, or Psalms, (carminum) David had composed. (Haydock) — Because. This was the chorus of the 135th Psalm, which was sung on this occasion by some, while others played on their musical instruments. (Calmet) — Literally, “singing by their hands.” Protestants, “when David praised by their ministry.” (Haydock) — As the temple was chosen by God for the place of prayer, so special forms of blessing, &c., are more agreeable to him. (Worthington)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Solemnity of tabernacles, (Menochius) which ensued after the dedication was ended."],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Day. He had given them leave to depart, the day before, 3 Kings viii. 66."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Face, to appease me, that I may look down upon them in mercy. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}